### C# SENSEI — V008 Wide Events
### Click "Send Request" above any block below. Works in Visual Studio 2022,
### Rider and the VS Code REST Client.
###
### Only {{$randomInt}} and {{$timestamp}} are used, because they are the two
### dynamic variables all three editors support. Rider additionally offers
### {{$random.integer(a, b)}}, {{$uuid}} and friends — nicer, but not portable.

### PORT: 5000 comes from Properties/launchSettings.json. If the console prints
### a different address on startup (running the built DLL, or a container, skips
### launchSettings), change the line below to match it.

@host = http://localhost:5000

###############################################################################
# 1. THE TWO SHOTS FROM THE VIDEO
#    Fixed values, so what you see matches what the lesson describes.
###############################################################################

### Small free-tier cart — fast, approved. The boring 95% of your traffic.
POST {{host}}/checkout
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userId": "u_10001",
  "tier": "free",
  "total": 42.00,
  "items": 2
}

### Large enterprise cart — slow, and likely declined.
### This is the request the whole lesson is about.
POST {{host}}/checkout
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userId": "u_88214",
  "tier": "enterprise",
  "total": 1299.99,
  "items": 7
}

###############################################################################
# 2. RANDOMISED SINGLE REQUESTS
#    Every send produces a new customer, which is what high cardinality
#    actually looks like in the data.
###############################################################################

### Random free-tier customer
POST {{host}}/checkout
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userId": "u_{{$randomInt}}",
  "tier": "free",
  "total": {{$randomInt}}.99,
  "items": 1
}

### Random business-tier customer
POST {{host}}/checkout
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userId": "u_{{$randomInt}}",
  "tier": "business",
  "total": {{$randomInt}}.50,
  "items": 3
}

### Random enterprise customer — expect this one to be slow
POST {{host}}/checkout
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userId": "u_{{$randomInt}}",
  "tier": "enterprise",
  "total": {{$randomInt}}.00,
  "items": 6
}

###############################################################################
# 3. SEED REAL VOLUME  ← run this before querying Azure
#
#    A percentile over nine requests is not a percentile. Send this once and
#    wait; 400 requests takes roughly 20-30 seconds because the simulated
#    gateway has deliberate latency.
#
#    /seed sends the traffic to whatever address you called it on, so it cannot
#    drift out of step with the port the app is actually listening on.
#
#    Then give Azure 2-5 minutes to ingest before running any KQL.
###############################################################################

### Seed 400 checkouts, 16 at a time (the default)
POST {{host}}/seed
Content-Type: application/json

### Seed a bigger sample — better percentiles, longer wait.
### 1500 takes a couple of minutes; raise your editor's request timeout first.
POST {{host}}/seed?count=1500&concurrency=32
Content-Type: application/json

### Quick smoke test — 20 requests, back in a few seconds
POST {{host}}/seed?count=20&concurrency=4
Content-Type: application/json

###############################################################################
# 4. EDGE CASES WORTH HAVING IN THE DATA
###############################################################################

### Right on the £500 boundary that changes the gateway's behaviour
POST {{host}}/checkout
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userId": "u_{{$randomInt}}",
  "tier": "enterprise",
  "total": 500.00,
  "items": 1
}

### Single high-value item
POST {{host}}/checkout
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userId": "u_{{$randomInt}}",
  "tier": "business",
  "total": 2400.00,
  "items": 1
}

### A repeat customer — send several times to build one person's history,
### which is what the "find one customer" query reads back.
POST {{host}}/checkout
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userId": "u_88214",
  "tier": "enterprise",
  "total": {{$randomInt}}.00,
  "items": 4
}

###############################################################################
# 5. THE BeginScope COMPARISON  (video section 10.5)
#
#    Same checkout, written with BeginScope and nested scopes instead of a
#    wide event. Watch the CONSOLE, not the response:
#
#      /checkout          -> ONE wide event, every field on one row
#      /checkout-scoped   -> FOUR decorated log lines, fields spread across them
#
#    And nothing from /checkout-scoped reaches customDimensions in Azure, so
#    none of the KQL above will find it.
###############################################################################

### The scoped version — four lines where the wide event writes one
POST {{host}}/checkout-scoped
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userId": "u_88214",
  "tier": "enterprise",
  "total": 1299.99,
  "items": 7
}

### The same payload through the wide-event endpoint, for comparison
POST {{host}}/checkout
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userId": "u_88214",
  "tier": "enterprise",
  "total": 1299.99,
  "items": 7
}
